<article class="post hentry">


<a href="/en/blog-details.html">


</a>


<header class="entry-header">


</header>


<div class="entry-content">


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cbt1.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: Vietnam.vn</div>


</div>


<h3>Introductory Article:</h3>


<h4>• Introduction:</h4>


<p>


    When you visit Ho Chi Minh City and want to find a traditional market just to enjoy the food, specialty goods and thereby understand the cultural life of the local people (people here what to eat, what to wear or what to use every day) then visit Ben Thanh market. This more than 100-year-old market is considered a cultural symbol of the city, voted in the top of the best markets in the world, ranked 2nd in the top 10 destinations with the most attractive street food on the planet.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cbt2.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: nhandan.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• History and name:</h4>


<p>


    •	Ben Thanh Market is one of the oldest historical markets in Ho Chi Minh City. Initially, around the 17th century, the market was built of bricks, wooden slopes and thatched roofs, located next to a river wharf near Gia Dinh citadel. This wharf was used to welcome visitors and soldiers into the citadel, so it was called Ben Thanh (i.e. landing before entering the citadel), and so the market was also called Ben Thanh market.<br/>


•	However, by mid-1911, the market was in disrepair and was in danger of collapsing. To avoid the risk, the French chose a nearby area and built a new, more stable market. That is the location of Ben Thanh market so far.<br/>


•	Over hundreds of years of history with many renovations, but Ben Thanh market still retains the old architecture and is considered as a typical symbol of Saigon that everyone wants to discover once coming to this city.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cbt3.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: alodi.net</div>


</div>


<h4>• Values:</h4>


<p>


    Since its inauguration, Ben Thanh Market has overwhelmed contemporary residents by the huge size of the market houses and a strikingly high clock tower. The newspaper Luc Tinh Tan Van at that time reported the feelings of small business households who had just come to trade in the new market, with an excited mood because of the spaciousness, openness and cleanliness of this project. Over hundreds of years, the architecture of the market still retains its spaciousness and coolness. Not only that, the beauty of the market is also enhanced by the bas-relief steps imbued with the traditional culture of Bien Hoa ceramic artists that are hung at the main gates of the market.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cbt4.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: danhsach.vn</div>


</div>


<h4>• Structure:</h4>


<p>


    •	Ben Thanh market design 4 main doors bordering 4 streets and 12 side doors radiating to 4 directions. With 1500 stalls and 6,000 small traders, the market sells many items for visitors to visit or enjoy on the spot.<br/>


•	“Cửa Nam” (main gate) located on Le Loi street, is a place to sell fabrics, clothes, dry food. In particular, where the South Gate also houses a 3-sided clock tower, which has been preserved since its inception, attracting many people to check-in and take photos.<br/>


•	“Cửa Bắc” is located on Le Thanh Ton Street, leading to fresh food, fruits, and dining.<br/>


•	“Cửa Đông” is located on Phan Boi Chau Street, a paradise of confectionery<br/>


•	“Cửa Tây” is located on Phan Chu Trinh Street, focusing on shoe stores, fine art goods and souvenirs.


  </p>


<div class="post-frame">


<img alt="Post image" src="/images/cbt5.jpg"/>


<div class="img-ref">Source: thanglong.chinhphu.vn</div>


</div>


</div>


</article>


